{% extends "page.j2" %}
{% set active_page = 'about' %}
{% set page_title = "About the contest" %}
{% set page_desc = "Rules, scoring, and divisions for NC(J)MO." %}
{% block content %}
    <nav class="page-links" aria-label="On this page"><a class="text-link" href="#rules-scoring">Rules &amp; scoring</a><a class="text-link" href="#divisions">Divisions</a><a class="text-link" href="#common-questions">Questions</a></nav>
    <section class="section-space rules-section" id="rules-scoring" aria-labelledby="rules-title">
        <div class="prose"><h2 class="section-title" id="rules-title">Rules &amp; scoring</h2><p id="contest-format">NC(J)MO follows a BAMO-style proof-based format. Both divisions have five problems and a three-hour time limit.</p></div>
        <div class="rules-grid">
            <article class="rule-card"><h3>7 points per problem</h3><p>Solutions are graded on completeness, clarity, and correctness. With five problems, the maximum score is 35 points.</p></article>
            <article class="rule-card"><h3>Show your reasoning</h3><p>Write proofs that explain how you reached your conclusions. Submit any progress you have, even if you have not fully solved a problem.</p></article>
            <article class="rule-card"><h3>Allowed tools</h3><p>You may use writing utensils, erasers, and scratch paper. A straightedge or ruler and a compass are optional.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="rules-source">See the original scoring instructions in the <a href="/static/2025/NCJMO-2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-describedby="new-tab-note">2025 NCJMO paper</a> and <a href="/static/2025/NCMO-2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-describedby="new-tab-note">2025 NCMO paper</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="two-column section-space" aria-labelledby="our-story">
        <div class="prose">
            <h2 class="section-title" id="our-story">Why this contest?</h2>
            <p>
                When you hear the phrase “math competition,” you’ll probably think of a fast-paced,
                high-pressure short-answer test that rewards accuracy, intuition, and speed.
                Although that has its own excitement, there is also something special
                about being able to forget about time and pressure and simply wrestle with a difficult problem:
                staring out the window as you let your mind wander through the craziest ideas before a solution clicks.
            </p>
            <p>
                We have met so many of you who are creative and ingenious,
                and can think deeply and rigorously about difficult problems,
                but don’t quite thrive in fast-paced, high-pressure environments.
                Although competitions like the USA(J)MO encourage deep and rigorous thinking,
                virtually all of these are only accessible at the highest levels,
                standing behind the walls of short-answer tests such as the AMCs and AIME.
                Our mission is to make these proof-based olympiads accessible to the community.
            </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="info-panel" aria-labelledby="contest-practice">
            <h2 class="section-title" id="contest-practice">Past problems</h2>
            <p>Browse both divisions’ tests, solutions, and results to see the level and style of the questions.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/archive">Browse the archive <svg class="arrow-icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path d="M4 16 16 4M4 4h12v12"/></svg></a>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section-space" id="divisions" aria-labelledby="choose-division">
        <div class="prose">
            <h2 class="section-title" id="choose-division">Choose your division</h2>
            <p>You can get a feeling for the difficulty by exploring <a href="/archive">problems from previous years</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="division-grid">
            <article class="division-card">
                <h3>NCJMO (Junior)</h3>
                <p>
                    If you haven’t quite explored beyond school math,
                    or tasted competition math beyond the AMCs, we encourage you to take the NCJMO.
                </p>
                <p>The difficulty is similar to BAMO-8. Explore past NCJMO problems to see what to expect.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="division-card">
                <h3>NCMO (Olympiad)</h3>
                <p>
                    If you’ve comfortably qualified for the AIME
                    or have experience with proof writing, such as from an advanced math course,
                    you should challenge yourself with the NCMO.
                </p>
                <p>The difficulty is approximately USAJMO level, with a couple of easier problems at the beginning and potentially a particularly challenging one at the end.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-space prose" aria-labelledby="common-questions">
        <h2 class="section-title" id="common-questions">Common questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
            <details>
                <summary>Proofs seem hard! Should I take the NC(J)MO?</summary>
                <p>
                    First things first, don’t be intimidated by the word “proof”!
                    Think of it as a fancy way of saying, “explain your thought process as clearly as you can.”
                    We encourage you to take the NC(J)MO even if you’re inexperienced!
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does NC(J)MO have the same difficulty as USA(J)MO?</summary>
                <p>
                    NCJMO is similar in difficulty to BAMO-8. NCMO is approximately USAJMO level,
                    with a couple of easier problems at the beginning and potentially a particularly challenging one at the end.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
